%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: TA collaborative learning
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: Which learning theory provides the primary foundation for collaborative
      learning?
    Options:
    - Behaviorism
    - Social Constructivism
    - Cognitivism
    - Connectivism
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'In collaborative learning, the term "positive interdependence"
      refers to:'
    Options:
    - Students working independently on the same task
    - The belief that one's success is linked to others' success
    - Competition among group members
    - Individual accountability being eliminated
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'A professor teaching "Philosophical Foundations of Education"
      divides students into groups to analyze different educational philosophies
      and present their findings. This approach primarily demonstrates:'
    Options:
    - Expository learning
    - Collaborative learning
    - Programmed instruction
    - Mastery learning
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is NOT a essential element of formal collaborative
      learning?
    Options:
    - Positive interdependence
    - Individual accountability
    - Promotive interaction
    - Independent work without communication
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The Jigsaw technique in collaborative learning was developed by:'
    Options:
    - Elliot Aronson
    - Lev Vygotsky
    - John Dewey
    - David Johnson
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When implementing collaborative learning in "Sociological Foundations
      of Education," what is the primary role of the teacher?
    Options:
    - Sole information provider
    - Facilitator and guide
    - Passive observer
    - Disciplinarian
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: Which of the following best describes "individual accountability"
      in collaborative learning?
    Options:
    - The group receives one grade for all members
    - Each member is responsible for their learning and contribution
    - The brightest student does all the work
    - No one is held responsible for the outcome
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The Zone of Proximal Development theory by Vygotsky is particularly
      relevant to collaborative learning because it emphasizes:'
    Options:
    - Independent problem-solving ability
    - What learners can achieve with guidance from peers
    - Fixed intelligence levels
    - Individual competition
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'In collaborative learning, "group processing" refers to:'
    Options:
    - Reflecting on how well the group is functioning
    - Individual members working separately
    - The teacher directing all activities
    - Competitive evaluation among groups
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: Which cooperative learning structure involves students becoming
      "experts" on different aspects of a topic and then teaching their peers?
    Options:
    - Think-Pair-Share
    - Jigsaw
    - Round Robin
    - Numbered Heads Together
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: A teacher educator notices that in collaborative groups for "Psychological
      Foundations," some students dominate while others remain passive. What strategy
      would best address this issue?
    Options:
    - Assigning specific roles to each group member
    - Allowing natural leaders to emerge
    - Eliminating group work entirely
    - Having only individual assignments
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'Compared to competitive learning approaches, collaborative learning
      in foundation courses is more effective for developing:'
    Options:
    - Individual ranking systems
    - Social skills and higher-order thinking
    - Standardized test performance only
    - Independent work habits exclusively
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When designing collaborative activities for "Historical Foundations
      of Education," what factor is most crucial for ensuring equal participation?
    Options:
    - Clear structure and defined roles
    - Allowing students to self-select groups
    - Having no time limits
    - Using only verbal tasks
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Think-Pair-Share" technique is particularly effective in
      foundation courses because it:'
    Options:
    - Allows only high-achieving students to participate
    - Gives all students time to process and articulate ideas
    - Requires no teacher preparation
    - Eliminates the need for individual thinking
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'A key challenge in implementing collaborative learning for large
      teacher education classes is:'
    Options:
    - Ensuring individual accountability in large groups
    - The method works only for small classes
    - Students prefer lecture methods
    - It requires less teacher preparation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: In collaborative learning, what is the primary purpose of "face-to-face
      promotive interaction"?
    Options:
    - To create social friendships
    - To help each other learn through explanation and support
    - To compete for teacher attention
    - To complete work more quickly
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When assessing collaborative learning in "Contemporary Indian Education,"
      what approach would be most fair and effective?
    Options:
    - Giving the same grade to all group members regardless of contribution
    - Combining group scores with individual assessment
    - Basing grades only on the final product
    - Evaluating only the group leader's work
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Numbered Heads Together" strategy is designed to ensure:'
    Options:
    - Only one student per group needs to understand the material
    - Equal participation and accountability from all members
    - Competition between group members
    - Independent work without collaboration
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: A professor wants to use collaborative learning for teaching complex
      concepts in "Educational Sociology." What preliminary step is most important?
    Options:
    - Ensuring students have necessary background knowledge
    - Letting students choose their own groups randomly
    - Providing no instructions to encourage creativity
    - Having students work completely independently first
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: Collaborative learning aligns with which educational philosophy's
      emphasis on social interaction and community?
    Options:
    - Essentialism
    - Perennialism
    - Progressivism
    - Behaviorism
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: In a collaborative learning activity on "Philosophical Foundations,"
      one group consistently produces superficial work. What intervention would be
      most appropriate?
    Options:
    - Providing more structured guidelines and checkpoints
    - Allowing them to continue without intervention
    - Assigning individual work instead
    - Reducing the complexity of the task significantly
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Structured Academic Controversy" technique in collaborative
      learning is particularly valuable for foundation courses because it:'
    Options:
    - Avoids all disagreement among students
    - Teaches students to engage with multiple perspectives
    - Ensures everyone agrees on one answer
    - Eliminates the need for research
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When forming groups for collaborative learning in "Psychological
      Foundations," what grouping strategy is supported by research?
    Options:
    - Homogeneous grouping by ability
    - Heterogeneous grouping with diversity in skills and backgrounds
    - Random grouping without consideration of student characteristics
    - Self-selected friendship groups only
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'A key benefit of collaborative learning for pre-service teachers
      is that it:'
    Options:
    - Models the teaching strategies they can use in their own classrooms
    - Requires less cognitive effort than individual work
    - Eliminates the need for teacher expertise
    - Always produces faster results than other methods
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'In collaborative learning, "social skills" that need explicit
      teaching include:'
    Options:
    - Only academic content knowledge
    - Leadership, communication, and conflict resolution
    - Individual research skills only
    - Test-taking strategies
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Group Investigation" model developed by Sharan and Sharan
      emphasizes:'
    Options:
    - Teacher-directed learning only
    - Students planning their own learning investigations
    - Individual competitive projects
    - Passive reception of information
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When implementing collaborative learning in foundation courses,
      what is the most common student resistance?
    Options:
    - Concerns about free-riding by some group members
    - Preference for listening to lectures
    - Lack of interest in social interaction
    - Desire for more homework
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Round Robin" brainstorming technique ensures:'
    Options:
    - Only the most vocal students participate
    - Equal opportunity for all group members to contribute ideas
    - The teacher controls all responses
    - Ideas are evaluated as they are shared
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'Collaborative learning is particularly effective for foundation
      courses because it:'
    Options:
    - Allows for diverse perspectives on complex educational concepts
    - Requires minimal teacher preparation time
    - Works only for simple, factual information
    - Eliminates the need for individual study
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: A professor notices that collaborative groups in "Historical Foundations"
      are discussing topics unrelated to the assignment. What strategy would best
      address this?
    Options:
    - Implementing regular check-ins and progress monitoring
    - Eliminating collaborative work entirely
    - Allowing off-topic discussions to continue
    - Punishing the entire class
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Learning Together" model developed by Johnson and Johnson
      emphasizes:'
    Options:
    - Competition between individuals
    - Five essential elements for effective collaboration
    - Independent learning without interaction
    - Teacher-centered instruction
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: In collaborative learning, what is the primary benefit of "peer
      teaching"?
    Options:
    - It reduces the teacher's workload significantly
    - Both the explainer and listener deepen their understanding
    - It ensures only correct information is shared
    - It eliminates the need for teacher expertise
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When assessing collaborative learning, what method best captures
      the process and product?
    Options:
    - Final exam only
    - Teacher observation without criteria
    - Peer evaluation only
    - Rubrics that evaluate both individual and group performance
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Three-Step Interview" technique in collaborative learning
      is particularly effective for:'
    Options:
    - Ensuring only one perspective is considered
    - Building interpersonal relationships and exploring content
    - Completing assignments quickly
    - Avoiding controversial topics
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'Collaborative learning in foundation courses helps develop "21st
      century skills" including:'
    Options:
    - Only content knowledge
    - Passive listening skills
    - Individual competition and ranking
    - Critical thinking, communication, and collaboration
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: A professor wants to implement collaborative learning in a diverse
      classroom with varying language proficiencies. What adaptation would be most
      effective?
    Options:
    - Using homogeneous grouping by language ability
    - Providing sentence starters and visual supports
    - Eliminating group work for these students
    - Having only native speakers present
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Pairs Check" strategy is designed to:'
    Options:
    - Encourage competition between partners
    - Provide immediate feedback and support
    - Reduce the amount of work completed
    - Eliminate the need for teacher assessment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'Research on collaborative learning shows it is particularly effective
      for:'
    Options:
    - High-achieving students only
    - Students from collectivist cultures only
    - Diverse student populations across achievement levels
    - Elementary students only
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When conflict arises in collaborative groups studying "Contemporary
      Educational Issues," what approach is most constructive?
    Options:
    - Ignoring the conflict hoping it resolves itself
    - Teaching conflict resolution strategies
    - Immediately reassigning students to new groups
    - Punishing students involved in conflicts
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Gallery Walk" technique in collaborative learning allows
      students to:'
    Options:
    - Work in isolation without interaction
    - Avoid presenting their work to others
    - Compete for the best individual product
    - View and provide feedback on multiple groups' work
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: In collaborative learning, what is the primary advantage of "heterogeneous
      grouping"?
    Options:
    - It ensures all students have similar ability levels
    - It provides diverse perspectives and peer scaffolding
    - It makes grouping decisions easier for teachers
    - It reduces the need for differentiated instruction
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: A professor using collaborative learning for "Sociological Foundations"
      notices some students are socially excluded. What proactive strategy would
      prevent this?
    Options:
    - Creating groups randomly each time
    - Ignoring social dynamics as irrelevant to learning
    - Allowing students to form exclusive friendship groups
    - Establishing group norms about inclusion
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Send a Problem" technique is particularly useful for:'
    Options:
    - Avoiding problem-solving activities
    - Generating multiple solutions to complex problems
    - Ensuring only one student does the work
    - Completing assignments without discussion
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'Collaborative learning requires careful planning of physical space
      to facilitate:'
    Options:
    - Individual isolated work
    - Teacher-centered instruction
    - Easy communication and interaction
    - Competitive environments
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When implementing collaborative learning in foundation courses,
      what timing consideration is most important?
    Options:
    - Providing sufficient time for groups to form, storm, norm, and perform
    - Rushing through activities to cover more content
    - Using the same time allocation for all activities
    - Allowing unlimited time with no deadlines
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Team Pair Solo" approach helps students by:'
    Options:
    - Starting with individual work only
    - Progressing from group support to independent work
    - Eliminating the need for individual accountability
    - Focusing only on social skills development
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'A key difference between collaborative learning and traditional
      group work is that collaborative learning:'
    Options:
    - Has structured interdependence and individual accountability
    - Allows some students to do all the work
    - Requires no planning or structure
    - Focuses only on the final product
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'In foundation courses, collaborative learning helps bridge theory
      and practice by:'
    Options:
    - Keeping theory abstract and separate from application
    - Allowing students to discuss how theories apply to real educational contexts
    - Focusing only on theoretical concepts
    - Eliminating the need for practical application
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: 'The "Fishbowl" technique in collaborative learning is particularly
      effective for:'
    Options:
    - Demonstrating group processes and modeling discussions
    - Keeping all discussions private
    - Ensuring only teacher-selected students participate
    - Avoiding peer observation and feedback
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
  - QuestionText: When evaluating the effectiveness of collaborative learning in
      foundation courses, what evidence would be most meaningful?
    Options:
    - Student satisfaction surveys only
    - Improvements in both academic achievement and social skills
    - Reduction in teacher workload
    - Speed of content coverage
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: TA collaborative learning
